    "result": "I implemented `RateLimiter` in `solution.py` following `spec.md`, keeping the given class and method signatures. Each client keeps a queue of `(time, cost)` entries; before every check, entries at least `window_seconds` old are dropped, so the budget in use is the sum of the live entries. `allow` records a request only when it is accepted. `remaining` and `retry_after` never record anything. `retry_after` walks the live entries oldest-first until enough units would have expired for the requested cost. A shared clock check rejects any `now` earlier than the largest time seen so far, for any client. Validation covers integer `limit` ≥ 1 and `cost` in 1..limit with `bool` rejected, and a positive `window_seconds`. I wrote 48 pytest tests in `tests/test_solution.py` covering validation, budget accounting, client isolation, the exact-window boundary, `retry_after` for single and multi-entry waits, and clock monotonicity. `python -m pytest -q tests` passes with 48 tests. I did not measure line coverage: the only shell command I was allowed to run was the test command, so I could not run a coverage tool. By reading the code, the tests exercise every branch except one defensive `return 0.0` that the spec makes unreachable. One test expectation was wrong at first (an entry exactly `window_seconds` old had expired), and I corrected the test, not the implementation.",
